        "elapsedMs": 12832,
        "text": "Absolutely—you previously mentioned in our DM that you enjoy **Adobe Premiere Pro** and are learning **Lumetri color grading**, so I’d start with these:\n\n| Resource | Best for | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Adobe Premiere tutorials](https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere-pro/tutorials.html) | Official lessons on editing, audio, color correction, and exporting; many include practice files. | Free tutorials; software subscription required to practice |\n| **Premiere Gal** on YouTube | Approachable Premiere walkthroughs covering captions, effects, sound, and editing workflows. | Free videos; optional paid resources |\n| **Justin Odisho** on YouTube | Short, focused Premiere tutorials—useful when you want to learn a particular effect or technique. | Free videos |\n| [Film Editing Pro](https://www.filmeditingpro.com/) | Editing craft: pacing, cutting to music, building tension, and telling a story—not just operating software. | Free tutorials and paid courses |\n| [LinkedIn Learning](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/) | Structured Premiere courses you can follow from beginning to end. Search for “Premiere Pro Essential Training.” | Paid; some libraries provide access |\n| [Frame.io Insider](https://blog.frame.io/) | Deeper articles on editing workflows, color management, audio, and professional post-production. | Free |\n\n**For color grading specifically:** start with Adobe’s lessons on **Lumetri Color and video scopes**. Learn exposure and white-balance correction before experimenting with LUTs or stylized looks.\n\n**A practical first project:** make a 30–60-second video using five to ten clips. Work through it in separate passes: story and cuts → sound → color → captions and export. You’ll learn more by finishing one small edit than by watching lots of disconnected tutorials.\n\nAre you most interested in editing **social media videos, cinematic short films, or personal/travel footage**?"
